// A fixture for `langc fmt`: parentheses the author wrote are kept.
//
// They do not survive into the AST -- `(a && b) || c` and `a && b || c` are
// one tree -- so the formatter is told where they were, the way it is told
// how each literal was spelled. It used to print the minimum precedence
// needed, which turned apps/git's SHA-1 rounds and apps/tui's `(x & M) != 0`
// into lines a reader has to check against the precedence table.
const int BOLD = 1;
int a=1; int b=2; int c=3; int d=4; int bb=5; int off=3;
// mixed && and ||: the grouping is the point
print(  (a>0 && b>0) || (c>0&&d>0)  );
// FIPS 180-4, round by round
print(d^(bb&(c^d)));
print((bb&c)|(d&(bb|c)));
// a mask test
print((off&BOLD)!=0);
// redundant, and still the author's -- but a group around a group is one group
print((a));
print(((a)));
print((a+b)+c);
print(a+(b+c));
// what precedence requires, the formatter still adds
print((a+b)*c);
print(-(a-b));
print((a+b).to_str());
// and where nothing was written, nothing appears
print(a+b*c);
print(a>0&&b>0||c>0);
